 "K3": {
  "question": "How can a website stop another site from forging the callback to its redirect page?",
  "answer1": "The client MUST implement CSRF protection for its redirection URI. This is typically accomplished by requiring any request sent to the redirection URI endpoint to include a value that binds the request to the user-agent's authenticated state (e.g., a hash of the session cookie used to authenticate the user-agent). (Section 10.12)",
  "answer2": "A website can use the state parameter, which SHOULD be used for preventing cross-site request forgery as described in Section 10.12. This parameter is an opaque value used by the client to maintain state between the request and callback. The authorization server includes this value when redirecting the user-agent back to the client. By verifying the state parameter, the client can ensure that the callback is not forged.",
